# The servo-side motion profile, loaded once at process setup and read by both
# control cogs.
#
# Protobuf text of `brenn_reachy.cogs.config_clk_proto.ServoProfile`, which the
# compiler generates from the `ServoProfile` schema in `config.clk`. Read as it
# is: the box binds it by path, and the casing converts it to the message the
# dial hands the cog.
#
# One file, two readers. The session writes these pairs into the nine servos
# when it commissions the machine; the decision tick builds its plant models
# from the same pairs, so what it judges a joint against is the trajectory the
# machine's own generators are running. A second statement of any of these
# numbers is a screen sized for a machine nobody commissioned.
#
# One pair per servo class and one loop constant beside it, stated as nine flat
# keys. The classes are separate
# because their motors and their Velocity Limits are: the antennas' Velocity
# Limit register reads 1620 and the seven head servos' reads 445, so what the
# antennas can be commissioned with is not what the head can. Flat keys rather
# than a nested pair message because every reader here is a first-match
# `key: value` line scan.

# The servo-side motion profile the commission sequencer writes, in the
# registers' own units (0.229 rev/min for velocity, 214.577 rev/min² for
# acceleration).
#
# Three pairs across the three classes, each read off the machine.
#
# The legs run their own capability: a tour of the whole clip library at
# `(32767, 445)` -- the generator wide open, so the motor and not the generator
# was the binding constraint -- read the class motor-bound at 326 velocity units
# with a goal-step ramp median of 287, and a confirmation tour of the same
# library at 287 / 326 held the six cranks 0.1983 rad from their own modelled
# trajectory at the worst, against a 0.4 rad bound, reading the class
# motor-bound at 326 again.
#
# The antennas run their own capability too: a tour at `(32767, 1620)` read the
# class motor-bound at 640 velocity units with a goal-step ramp median of 522,
# and a tour of the whole library at 522 / 640 stands 0.2536 rad from the
# modelled trajectory at the worst once the loop's own following lag is in the
# model -- 0.5078 rad against the generator alone, which is what kept the pair
# on record and uncommissioned until the lag was measured. Two sweeps and two
# step probes at the pair, armed, read 0.2051-0.2535 rad behind and
# 0.1913-0.2152 rad ahead against a 0.4 rad bound, and the same tour reads the
# class motor-bound with a plateau of 675 against the 640 commissioned.
#
# The body yaw runs at its motor's own limit: velocity 445 is the head servos'
# Velocity Limit register, and on the derivative gain ladder's two quiet rungs
# (800 / 0 / 400 and 400 / 0 / 400, generator wide open) the shaft cruised at
# that register on every long move -- 441-451 units a period on the step and
# hold probes. Acceleration 317 is the smaller of the two step probes' ramp
# medians at 800 / 0 / 400 (317 and 328). A library tour at 317 / 445, armed,
# read the yaw's worst residual at 0.2184 rad against a 0.4 rad bound, with no
# raise. The capability instrument's regime lines on the step probes are not
# what the pair was read from: its band table bins each goal step's ramp,
# TODO(capability-ramp-bands).
#
# The bench's trial-validated figures (400/600, including an 855°/s antenna
# sweep and the 0.8 s presence gesture) were sized for a host commanding whole
# moves, where the profile is only the backstop under host-side shaping and a
# tight one throttles the shaped motion itself. The session streams
# step-bounded setpoints instead, one per cycle, so a class's pair is read off
# what its own motor does rather than off what a host wanted headroom for.
#
# Zero in either register is a servo with no generator at all. Both readers
# refuse it, per class, before anything is commanded.
#
# The following lags are not registers and are written to no servo: each is a
# measurement of how far a class's position loop stands behind the trajectory
# it is following, which is what lets the tick predict a healthy joint rather
# than its generator. A lag is read by scanning it over a kept recording for
# the figure that minimises the class's p99.9 residual, and accepted only where
# two recordings at different profile pairs agree on it. A lag is a property of
# the loop, so it is read at a gains triple and a change of gains re-reads it.
#
# The legs' 24 000 us is 1.2 periods of the 20 ms grid, read at gains
# 800 / 100 / 300 off the two 2026-09-09 tours whose legs ran motor-bound at
# 287 / 326; both scans minimise at 1.2 and each cuts the class's p99.9
# residual by over half. The antennas' 24 000 us is read at gains 200 / 0 / 0
# off the same day's tours at 522 / 640 and 418 / 512, which minimise at 1.2
# and 1.3 -- inside the quarter period the agreement rule allows -- for 2.28x
# and 1.83x. The body yaw is zero because it has no reading: two step probes at
# 317 / 445 and 317 / 333 read motor-bound but not as a following-lag
# instrument, so the scans did not agree. Zero is the generator alone.
legs_profile_acceleration: 287
legs_profile_velocity: 326
body_yaw_profile_acceleration: 317
body_yaw_profile_velocity: 445
antennas_profile_acceleration: 522
antennas_profile_velocity: 640
legs_following_lag_us: 24000
body_yaw_following_lag_us: 0
antennas_following_lag_us: 24000
